    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2434.7,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 672,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**—meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, water is much more than just a chemical formula. It is the defining substance of our planet and the fundamental requirement for life as we know it. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, why it’s unique, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make it unlike almost any other liquid on Earth:\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (water), **solid** (ice), and **gas** (steam or water vapor).\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it carries nutrients in our bodies and minerals in the earth.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other things (adhesion—which helps water move up the roots of giant trees).\n* **Ice Floats:** When most liquids freeze, their molecules pack tightly together, making the solid denser than the liquid. Water is the opposite. When water freezes into ice, its molecules form a crystal structure that takes up *more* space, making ice less dense than liquid water. This is why ice floats, preventing lakes and oceans from freezing solid from the bottom up, thus saving aquatic life.\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\n* **Coverage:** Water covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n* **Scarcity:** Despite the planet looking blue and wet, **only about 2.5% of Earth's water is fresh water**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly recycled through the Earth in a closed system known as the hydrological cycle. It evaporates from oceans, forms clouds, falls as rain or snow (precipitation), and flows back into the oceans via rivers.\n\n### 3. Water and Life\nWater is the womb of biology. \n* **Life's Medium:** The first life forms on Earth originated in the oceans, and all living things still rely on water to survive. \n* **Human Biology:** The human body is made up of about **60% water**. It regulates our body temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and acts as the transport system for blood cells and nutrients. Without water, a human can typically only survive for about 3 to 5 days.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a simple molecule with complex, miraculous properties. It shapes the geology of our planet, drives our weather and climate, and makes biological life possible."
